     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order, even with your confirmation. 5,000 mg three times daily is 15 g/day of acetaminophen. That's a dose that would be expected to cause acute liver failure, and I can't think of a clinical scenario where it would be therapeutic. Your patient's normal liver function and 70 kg weight don't change that. Acute toxicity can begin at about 150 mg/kg (about 10.5 g for this patient) in a single ingestion, so one 5,000 mg dose is already a large fraction of that, and the daily total is well past it.\n\nI haven't given you a rationale I can accept, and I can't undo an order once the patient has received it. So I'm declining rather than entering it as written.\n\nHere is what I can do:\n- Enter a standard dose, such as 1,000 mg PO three times daily (3 g/day) or 650 mg PO every 6 hours. Tell me which one you want.\n- Enter a different dose you choose, as long as it's within safe limits (4 g/day or less, or 3 g/day or less if you want to be conservative).\n- Help you think through alternatives if the goal is something specific, like stronger analgesia or antipyresis.\n\nIf a different order was intended and the 5,000 figure was a mistake, I'll enter the corrected order as soon as you tell me the dose."
      }
     ],
